[38;5;231m⏺[39m Committed [38;5;153m22dad6d[39m on [38;5;153mmain[39m — the off-by-one fix only. Tests pass (2/2).

  My calls on the other two, and why:

  [1mKeeping [38;5;153mtime.monotonic()[39m.[0m Wall-clock time is adjustable; the window's correctness depends on timestamps that only move
  forward at a fixed rate. An NTP step backwards leaves expired events in the deque and rejects traffic that should
  pass; a step forwards flushes the window early and lets a burst through. Both are silent, unreproducible in tests, and
  hit exactly when you least want a rate limiter misbehaving. The benefit on offer — timestamps that read the same as
  log lines — is a debugging convenience, and it's obtainable without putting the wall clock in the control path: log
  [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m at the call site, or store [38;5;153m(monotonic, wall)[39m pairs and evict on the first while reporting the second.
  Trading a correctness property for a formatting convenience isn't a trade I'd make, so I left the clock and its
  docstring rationale alone. If you want the [38;5;153m(monotonic, wall)[39m pairing so the limiter can emit correlatable times, say
  so and I'll add it — that one's cheap and costs nothing in correctness.

  [1mNot adding the storage-backend interface.[0m There's no second implementation to design against: no Redis in the repo, no
  distributed deployment, one call site. The deeper problem is that a Redis limiter isn't a deque behind a
  [38;5;153mget/append/evict[39m protocol — it needs the evict-count-append sequence to be atomic server-side (Lua, or
  [38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m+[38;5;153mZADD[39m in a MULTI), otherwise concurrent processes race past the limit. And monotonic time is
  per-process, so a shared backend needs a different clock story entirely. An interface extracted from today's in-memory
  code would encode assumptions Redis breaks, and would get rewritten at the moment it was finally used — so it buys
  nothing now and doesn't reduce the later work. The extraction is easy to do later, with a real implementation in hand
  to shape it.

  Both of these are reversible if the context is different from what I can see — a planned Redis migration, or a
  specific incident where the monotonic timestamps blocked a debug session, would change my answer.

